  "image_name": "1b11ffc68c6acb31e7bf2a5883cd1bd2.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several roles of caveolin-1 (CAV1) and how its membrane-associated structure helps organize signaling. Overall, it links CAV1 to regulation of endothelial nitric oxide synthase (eNOS), mitochondrial effects of nitric oxide, and membrane targeting of signaling proteins through lipid modifications.\n\nAt the top, CAV1 is shown binding eNOS and suppressing nitric oxide (NO) production through the caveolin scaffolding domain. NO is then depicted acting on mitochondria, where it appears to interfere with respiratory complexes and contribute to peroxynitrite formation, while also competing with oxygen.\n\nThe larger lower schematic emphasizes CAV1 as an integral plasma-membrane protein with a hairpin-like membrane insertion, oligomerization region, and scaffolding domain. Multiple palmitate chains and an N-terminal myristate are highlighted, showing how lipid anchors help recruit signaling partners. One example is SRC, whose membrane association seems to depend on myristoylation, SH2/SH3-containing interactions, and phosphorylation at Tyr14. A parallel example shows a GPI-anchored protein outside the membrane and a doubly acylated protein on the cytoplasmic side, suggesting CAV1-rich membrane domains concentrate both extracellular and intracellular lipid-anchored proteins. Together, the image conveys CAV1 as a membrane organizer and negative regulator of specific signaling pathways.",
